\section{The asymptotic manifold, measured}
\label{sec:manifold}

Item~2 of \S\ref{sec:cannot} is a genuine limitation of the support graph, but
it is not a limitation of the model: the split it cannot see is computable
exactly at small $N$, and this section computes it. Doing so changes the
reading of two of the rules \S\ref{sec:regimes} classified.

\subsection{What the two factors are}

Write the structure theorem \eqref{eq:aj} out with its physical content
attached. Every state converges into
\begin{equation}
  \rho(\infty) \;=\; \bigoplus_k \; p_k \;\, \rho_k \otimes \sigma_k ,
  \qquad
  \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{asym}} = \bigoplus_k \mathcal{H}_{n_k} \otimes
  \mathcal{H}_{m_k},
  \label{eq:manifold}
\end{equation}
and the three objects are three different kinds of thing.

\begin{itemize}
\item $p_k = \Tr(J_k \rho_0)$ is a \emph{classical label}, fixed at $t = 0$ and
never moving again. This is the part the population graph sees.
\item $\rho_k$ is an arbitrary state on an $n_k$-dimensional factor. It is not
damped at all; the channel acts on it as a unitary, $\rho_k \mapsto U_k \rho_k
U_k^\dagger$. Everything written here survives forever. This is a
\emph{decoherence-free subspace} in the sense of Zanardi and
Rasetti~\cite{zr1997}, a noiseless subsystem in the sense of Knill, Laflamme
and Viola~\cite{klv2000}.
\item $\sigma_k$ is a \emph{fixed} full-rank state on an $m_k$-dimensional
factor, determined by the channel alone. Whatever is written there is erased
and replaced by $\sigma_k$. It is junk.
\end{itemize}

The set of steady states is convex and compact, and its \emph{extreme points}
--- the states that are not mixtures of other steady states --- are read off
\eqref{eq:manifold} directly: $p$ concentrated on one $k$, and $\rho_k =
|\psi\rangle\langle\psi|$ with $\psi$ inside a single eigenspace of $U_k$. Two
consequences are worth stating because they are easy to get backwards.

\begin{remark}
An extremal steady state is usually a \emph{mixed} state of the full system:
it is $|\psi\rangle\langle\psi|\otimes\sigma_k$ and $\sigma_k$ has full rank on
$m_k$ dimensions. Extremality is convex geometry, not purity.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
There are either finitely many extremal steady states or a continuum, never
anything between. If every $n_k = 1$ there are exactly $K$ of them and the
steady-state set is a simplex with $K$ vertices --- one per attractor, the
classical picture. As soon as some $n_k \ge 2$ the extremals contain a copy of
$\mathbb{CP}^{\,n_k-1}$ and the steady-state set grows a Bloch-ball face. That
jump is the difference between a classical memory and a quantum one, and
$n_{\mathrm{rec}}$ cannot detect it.
\end{remark}

\subsection{How it is computed}

Exactly, with no time-stepping. The Ces\`aro projector at eigenvalue $1$ is
built from the left and right kernels of $S - \mathbb{1}$, where $S$ is the
dense $4^N$ one-cycle superoperator; applied to $\mathbb{1}/d$ it gives
$\rho_\infty$, whose support \emph{is} $\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{asym}}$, of
dimension $D = \sum_k n_k m_k$. That support is invariant, so the channel
restricts to it --- and the restricted channel has a \emph{full-rank} fixed
state, which is exactly the hypothesis under which the peripheral
eigenoperators ($|\lambda| = 1$) of the dual map close into a $*$-algebra
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{A} \;=\; \bigoplus_k M_{n_k} \otimes \mathbb{1}_{m_k},
  \qquad \dim\mathcal{A} = \sum_k n_k^2 ,
  \label{eq:asymalg}
\end{equation}
on which the dual map acts as an automorphism. Splitting $\mathcal{A}$ by its
centre gives the blocks; the rank of each block's subalgebra gives $n_k$ and
the block support gives $m_k$.

Two points of technique. The \emph{fixed} points of $\Phid$ are not enough:
whenever $U_k \ne \mathbb{1}$ they give only the commutant of $U_k$, which for
any unitary rule collapses \eqref{eq:asymalg} to something far smaller than the
truth. And the peripheral subspace must be taken from a \emph{sorted Schur
form}, not from eigenvectors: with a degenerate peripheral spectrum
\rt{numpy.linalg.eig} returns nearly parallel eigenvectors --- for $W_{51}$ at
$N=5$ all $1024$ eigenvalues lie on the circle but the eigenvector matrix has
numerical rank $1001$ --- while a Schur basis of an invariant subspace is
orthonormal by construction.

\subsection{The two pure cases, and one that has both}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The asymptotic manifold at $N = 4$, obc0. $D$ is its dimension,
$\dim\mathcal{A} = \sum_k n_k^2$, and $\dim\mathrm{Fix}(\Phid) < \dim\mathcal{A}$
exactly when some $U_k \neq \mathbb{1}$.}
\label{tab:blocks}
\begin{tabular}{rlrrrl}
\toprule
rule & word & $D$ & $\dim\mathcal{A}$ & $\dim\mathrm{Fix}$ & blocks \\
\midrule
$W_{232}$ & DIIE & 7  & 49  & 49 & $M_7\otimes\sigma_1$ \\
$W_{36}$  & IDDV & 6  & 36  & 36 & $M_6\otimes\sigma_1$ \\
$W_{203}$ & VEII & 4  & 16  & 10 & $M_4\otimes\sigma_1$ \\
$W_{150}$ & IVVI & 16 & 256 & 36 & $M_{16}\otimes\sigma_1$ \\
$W_{91}$  & VEED & 3  & 1   & 1  & $M_1\otimes\sigma_3$ \\
$W_{189}$ & EIVE & 7  & 2   & 2  & $M_1\otimes\sigma_4 \;\oplus\; M_1\otimes\sigma_3$ \\
$W_{19}$  & VVVD & 13 & 17  & 9  & $M_4\otimes\sigma_1 \;\oplus\; M_1\otimes\sigma_9$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{$m_k = 1$: a pure decoherence-free subspace.} $W_{232}$ = DIIE is,
decoded back to an elementary cellular automaton, $f(l,s,r) = \mathrm{majority}$.
Its asymptotic algebra is the \emph{full} matrix algebra $M_7$ with $U_k =
\mathbb{1}$: nothing is erased, the asymptotic manifold is every state on a
seven-dimensional subspace, and the extremal steady states form a continuum
$\mathbb{CP}^6$. The population graph reports seven fixed points and calls them
seven classical pointer states; the truth is $\log_2 7 \approx 2.8$
\emph{qubits} held exactly. This reproduces, from the superoperator, the count
$\dim\mathcal{A} = n_{\mathrm{rec}}^2 = 49, 121$ at $N = 4, 5$ that
\fn{permutation/coherence.py} obtains by an unrelated route (label
synchronisation along the orbit), which is a useful cross-check on both.

\paragraph{$n_k = 1$: pure mixing.} $W_{91}$ = VEED has a single block with $n =
1$, $m = 3$: one steady state, of rank three, with spectrum exactly
$(\tfrac25,\tfrac25,\tfrac15)$, reached from every initial state. No memory of
any kind; the manifold is a point. At $N = 5$ the manifold is a single
\emph{pure} state ($D = 1$), which is the $N \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ case of the
closed form for $W_{91}$'s largest recurrent class recorded in this project's
notes. $W_{189}$ = EIVE has two such blocks, $(1,4)$ and $(1,3)$: exactly two
extremal steady states, both mixed, and the manifold is the line segment
joining them. That is bistability in its purest form --- one classical bit
survives, no qubit does --- and it matches $n_{\mathrm{wcc}}(W_{189}) = 2$.

\paragraph{Both at once.} $W_{19}$ = VVVD carries one block of each kind,
$\mathbb{C}^4 \oplus (\mathbb{C}^1\otimes\mathbb{C}^9)$. Its
decoherence-free block \emph{rotates}: $\dim\mathrm{Fix} = 8 < 16$ and the
peripheral phases are $\{0,\pi\}$, so $U_0$ has two doubly degenerate
eigenvalues of opposite sign and the encoded state flips every cycle. The other
block has a unique rank-nine $\sigma$. The extremal steady states are two Bloch
spheres plus one isolated point.

\subsection{Two findings}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{No block mixes the two factors.} Across all $256$ rules at $N=4$
and again at $N=5$, obc0, not one block has $n_k > 1$ \emph{and} $m_k > 1$.
Every block is either a pure decoherence-free subspace or a single state. The
genuinely quantum intermediate case --- a noiseless \emph{subsystem}, where
information hides in a tensor factor and is invisible to any subspace
projection --- does not occur in this family at these sizes. At $N=4$: $141$
rules have some $n_k > 1$, $49$ have some $m_k > 1$, four rules
($W_{19}, W_{61}, W_{103}, W_{179}$) carry both kinds of block in the same
channel, and $70$ rules have every block trivial, of which $68$ have a unique
steady state. We record this as an observation, not a theorem; it is exactly
the kind of statement that could fail at larger $N$.

\item \textbf{Two of the ``multistable'' rules are not.} $W_{203}$ and $W_{36}$
are among the eight rules that motivated this report: $n_{\mathrm{wcc}} = 1$ and
$n_{\mathrm{wcc}} = 10$ against $n_{\mathrm{rec}} = 28$ and $60$ at $N = 10$,
with certain fractions of $46\%$ and $73\%$ (\S\ref{sec:measured}). Their
asymptotic algebras are $M_4$ and $M_6$ at $N=4$, and $M_5$ and $M_9$ at $N=5$
--- \emph{single} blocks. The channel's asymptotic evolution on them is
unitary; there are no competing attractors for noise to choose between. What
\S\ref{sec:measured} measured is a property of the \emph{monitored} chain, in
which the computational basis is dephased once per cycle, and that is the right
object for the questions \S\S\ref{sec:answer}--\ref{sec:bits} ask. But the
sentence ``the noise decides which attractor'' is a statement about the
monitored channel and must not be carried over to the unmonitored one. The
distinction is the same one R7 drew for the pair graph and the $X$-gate
correction drew for permutation circuits; it now has numbers on both sides.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Memory, and what it costs to correct errors}
\label{sec:qec}

\subsection{The asymptotic algebra is the code}

Equation~\eqref{eq:asymalg} is not merely analogous to an error-correcting code;
it is the same object. Blume-Kohout, Ng, Poulin and Viola~\cite{bknpv2008}
proved that every \emph{information-preserving structure} of a quantum process
--- and their notion subsumes noiseless subsystems, decoherence-free subspaces,
pointer bases and error-correcting codes --- is a matrix algebra, and is
isometric to the fixed-point algebra of an associated unital process. What our
$\mathcal{A}$ is, is that algebra for the channel iterated forever. So the R22
counts sort into three distinct kinds of resource:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
quantity & what it counts & code-theoretic name \\
\midrule
$n_{\mathrm{wcc}} = \dim(\mathcal{C}\cap\mathcal{D})$ & protected classical bits
  & zero-error classical capacity \\
$n_k$ in \eqref{eq:asymalg} & protected qubits
  & decoherence-free subspace / noiseless subsystem \\
$n_{\mathrm{rec}} - n_{\mathrm{wcc}}$ & nothing & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The third row is Theorem~\ref{prop:idem} restated in the language of codes.
Extra terminal components inside one weak component add conserved quantities,
but they add no conserved \emph{projections}, hence no distinguishable-with-
certainty codewords, hence nothing to the zero-error capacity. In the same
language, \S\ref{sec:bits}'s finding is that the ordinary (average-case)
capacity is extensive in both regimes: what multistability destroys is
\emph{zero-error} capacity specifically.

It is worth recording the operator-level statement behind that, which is
stronger than the diagonal version proved in Theorem~\ref{prop:idem}.

\begin{proposition}[Every conserved question is a strong symmetry]
\label{prop:projections}
Let $P = P^2 = P^\dagger$ satisfy $\Phid(P) = P$. Then $[P, K_j] = 0$ for every
$j$. Consequently $\mathcal{C}$ and $\mathcal{F}$ contain exactly the same
projections, and the entire gap $\mathcal{F}\setminus\mathcal{C}$ consists of
non-idempotent elements.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $\rho$ supported in $\operatorname{ran}P$ we get $\Tr(P\,\Phi(\rho)) =
\Tr(\Phid(P)\rho) = \Tr(P\rho) = 1$, so $\operatorname{ran}P$ is invariant. The
same computation with $\mathbb{1}-P$, which is also a conserved projection,
makes $\operatorname{ran}P^\perp$ invariant. Every $K_j$ therefore preserves
both summands of $\mathcal{H} = \operatorname{ran}P \oplus
\operatorname{ran}P^\perp$, i.e.\ is block-diagonal, i.e.\ commutes with $P$.
\end{proof}

This also explains why $\mathcal{F}$ is not an algebra while $\mathcal{C}$ is.
A commutant is closed under products by construction; $\mathcal{F}$ is closed
under products only when $\Phid$ is multiplicative on it, and $\Phid$ is a
$*$-automorphism precisely in the closed case $\Phid(A) = U^\dagger A U$. That
is \S\ref{sec:closed} seen from the algebraic side. The general criterion is
Frigerio's~\cite{frigerio1978}: with a \emph{faithful} stationary state,
$\mathcal{F}$ is a von Neumann algebra and coincides with $\mathcal{C}$. A large
transient region is exactly what forbids a faithful stationary state, so our
dissipative rules sit as far from that hypothesis as possible --- measured at
$N=4$, obc0, $(\dim\mathcal{C}, \dim\mathcal{F})$ is $(30,49)$ for $W_{232}$,
$(20,36)$ for $W_{36}$ and $(1,10)$ for $W_{203}$: one strong symmetry, ten
conserved quantities.

\subsection{Fragmentation as a source of logical qubits: Iadecola's construction}

Iadecola~\cite{iadecola2026} asks what fragmentation buys when it is combined
with a symmetry rather than considered alone. In a charge-conserving,
kinetically constrained chain, the projectors onto the Krylov sectors and the
generators of charge conjugation and translation generate a \emph{non-Abelian}
algebra, and the consequence is exponentially many logical qubits encoded in
degenerate pairs of eigenstates, which may be highly entangled.

The mechanism translates into our language cleanly, and the translation makes
the difference visible. His degeneracies come from a symmetry that \emph{maps
one fragment onto another}, so that the pair carries a two-dimensional
irreducible representation. In \eqref{eq:asymalg} that is precisely $n_k = 2$:
a non-Abelian commutant is the same thing as a block of dimension greater than
one, and this project has already met one --- R-T15 identified the single-$V$
commutant of $W_{108}/W_{201}/W_{156}/W_{198}$ as non-Abelian, of
Temperley--Lieb type.

Two differences matter.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Closed versus open.} Iadecola's setting is Hamiltonian, so there
is no transient part and $\mathcal{C} = \mathcal{F} = \mathcal{A}$; the
distinction this whole report is about does not arise. Ours has transients, and
they break the identification: $W_{203}$ at $N = 4$ has $\dim\mathcal{C} = 1$
--- no strong symmetry whatsoever --- and yet $\mathcal{A} = M_4$, a
four-dimensional decoherence-free subspace. \textbf{A decoherence-free subspace
need not be protected by any symmetry of the dynamics}; it can be a property of
the recurrent set alone, invisible in $\mathcal{C}$ because $\mathcal{C}$ must
also respect the transients.
\item \textbf{Where the coherence comes from.} His comes from a symmetry
relating sectors. Ours comes from the resets acting \emph{trivially} on the
recurrent set: a reset destroys $|x\rangle\langle y|$ only if it fires on a bit
where $x$ and $y$ differ, so once the dynamics has settled onto configurations
the resets never touch, all coherences among them survive. That is the $J$-label
criterion of the $X$-gate correction, and it is why $W_{232}$'s algebra is the
whole of $M_{n_{\mathrm{rec}}}$.
\end{enumerate}

A third point of contact is worth flagging as a caution rather than a result.
Iadecola derives from the same algebra \emph{necessary conditions} for the usual
experimental signatures of fragmentation, such as the late-time persistence of
density imbalances. Our $\S$\ref{sec:measured} numbers are exactly such
late-time observables, and Finding~2 of \S\ref{sec:manifold} is an instance of
his warning: a late-time imbalance measured in the computational basis reports
on the monitored chain, and can indicate multistability where the unmonitored
channel has a single coherent block.

\subsection{The majority rule as an autonomous memory}

Guedes, Winter and M\"uller~\cite{guedes2024} propose exactly this family of
automata as measurement-free quantum error correction: a QCA whose local update
is a classical density-classification rule, run as a decoder for a repetition
code, with no syndrome extraction anywhere. The two rules they study are
Wolfram's rule $232$ --- local majority voting --- and Toom's two-line voting in
two dimensions. Rule $232$ is \emph{our} $W_{232}$: decoding the gate word DIIE
through the project's encoding gives $(c_p,c_q) = (f(l,0,r), f(l,1,r))$ with
$f = \mathrm{majority}$, so the automaton whose asymptotic algebra
Table~\ref{tab:blocks} reports as $M_7$ is the one they run as a decoder.

That makes the comparison direct, and it is not flattering to fragmentation.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{$W_{232}$ = majority voting as an autonomous memory, obc0. $d$ is the
largest weight such that \emph{every} error of that weight or less is undone by
the automaton itself. The obc0 boundary pins the left and right ends to $0$, so
the all-ones codeword is unprotected at its edges and $d(1^N) = 0$; the
periodic counterpart is deliberately not run here.}
\label{tab:memory}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrrr}
\toprule
$N$ & attractors & basin $0^N$ & basin $1^N$ & code basin & $d(0^N)$ &
repetition $d$ \\
\midrule
 8 &    44 &   41 &   12 & $20.70\%$ & 1 & 3 \\
10 &   117 &   99 &   29 & $12.50\%$ & 1 & 4 \\
12 &   305 &  239 &   70 & $\phantom{0}7.54\%$ & 1 & 5 \\
14 &   798 &  577 &  169 & $\phantom{0}4.55\%$ & 1 & 6 \\
16 &  2091 & 1393 &  408 & $\phantom{0}2.75\%$ & 1 & 7 \\
18 &  5473 & 3363 &  985 & $\phantom{0}1.66\%$ & 1 & 8 \\
20 & 14328 & 8119 & 2378 & $\phantom{0}1.00\%$ & 1 & 9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three readings of Table~\ref{tab:memory}.

\paragraph{The automaton corrects exactly one error, at every size.} A single
flipped bit is an isolated minority and majority voting removes it. Two
\emph{adjacent} flipped bits are not: each of them sees one neighbour agreeing,
so the pair is stable, and it is stable because it is one of the automaton's own
protected pointer states. The autonomous distance is therefore $1$ for every
$N$, against a repetition-code distance of $\lfloor (N-1)/2 \rfloor$. This is
precisely the ``islands'' failure Guedes et al.\ identify, and R22's vocabulary
names its cause: the islands are not decoding failures, they are \emph{other
weak components}, and the automaton preserves them with the same fidelity with
which it preserves the codewords. The very property that makes $W_{232}$ a good
passive memory --- $\mathcal{A} = M_{n_{\mathrm{rec}}}$, everything protected
--- is what makes it a bad decoder.

\paragraph{The code basin shrinks exponentially.} The two codeword basins are
Pell and NSW numbers, growing like $(1+\sqrt2)^{N/2} \approx 1.554^N$ against a
state space of $2^N$, so the fraction of configurations the automaton returns to
a codeword decays like $0.777^N$: half the space at $N=4$, one percent at
$N=20$. Meanwhile the attractor count grows like $\varphi^N$. A decoder is
useful when the intended codewords have basins of measure approaching one; here
they have measure approaching zero.

\paragraph{Hence: autonomous error correction wants the \emph{opposite} of
fragmentation.} The figure of merit for a self-correcting memory is a
\emph{small} asymptotic algebra with \emph{large} basins --- ideally $K = 2$
blocks, or one block with $n_k = 2$, and a transient region covering
essentially the whole space. Exponential $n_{\mathrm{wcc}}$ means exponentially
many competing enclosures, so the intended codeword's basin is an exponentially
small fraction of the space and the automaton faithfully preserves errors. This
is the same obstruction that sends Guedes et al.\ to Toom's rule in two
dimensions, where the two consensus states have basins of measure approaching
one. Fragmentation is a good \emph{passive} memory --- it protects what is
already there --- and a bad \emph{active} one, because it protects the wrong
states just as well.

\paragraph{The absorption probabilities are the decoder.} One further
identification makes \S\ref{sec:measured} directly usable here. If the intended
codeword is terminal class $i$ and an error takes the state to $x$, then
$\varphi_i(x)$ of \eqref{eq:absorb} is exactly the probability that the
automaton restores the codeword: $\varphi$ \emph{is} the decoder's success
landscape, and the ``certain fraction'' reported in \S\ref{sec:measured} is the
fraction of configurations decoded with probability one. Read that way, the
$100\%$ for $W_{73}/W_{109}$ against $46\%$ for $W_{203}$ is a statement about
decoders, and Theorem~\ref{prop:idem} says the certain part is supported on
whole weak components and nowhere else.
